<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Nassau-Hadamar</h1><p class="st-meta">1607 CE – 1711 CE · capital Hadamar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Nassau principality in the Westerwald that became a Catholic outpost of the house and left a baroque residence town.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadamar">Hadamar</a> was created in the 1607 division of the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-count-of-nassau-dillenburg">John VI of Nassau-Dillenburg</a> and went to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-louis-prince-of-nassau-hadamar">John Louis</a>. John Louis converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1629 and served the emperor as a diplomat at the Peace of Westphalia, for which he was made a prince of the Empire in 1650.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After its ruler's conversion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadamar">Hadamar</a> became a Catholic enclave among Protestant <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau">Nassau</a> lands, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college and Franciscan friars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-louis-prince-of-nassau-hadamar">John Louis</a> rebuilt Hadamar as a planned residence town with a new palace. The economy was agrarian, with small-scale iron and wool working in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a> hills.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The line died out with Prince Francis Alexander in 1711, and the territory was divided among the other Nassau-Ottonian lines, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-diez">Nassau-Diez</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-nassau-siegen">Nassau-Siegen</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-nassau-dillenburg">Nassau-Dillenburg</a>. Its lands later passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a> and, in 1866, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1607 CE – 1711 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.68%;width:2.07%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1607 CE – 1711 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hadamar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Louis, Maurice Henry, Francis Alexander</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nassau-Hadamar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1659&amp;focus=principality-of-nassau-hadamar" aria-label="Principality of Nassau-Hadamar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4864 425 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Nassau-Hadamar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5184 681L5189 673"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5189 673h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5184 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5184 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hadamar. <a href="/atlas?year=1659&amp;focus=principality-of-nassau-hadamar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
